{"document_id":"aicis-retinal-evaluation-v1","title":"Retinal (Retinaldehyde) — AICIS evaluation factual card","source":"Australian Industrial Chemicals Introduction Scheme (AICIS)","source_family":"AICIS","source_url":"https://www.industrialchemicals.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-06/EVA00186%20-%20Evaluation%20statement%20-%2026%20June%202026.pdf","published_year":2026,"document_type":"regulatory-safety-provenance-card","evidence_tier":"regulatory-safety-provenance","chunk_text":"Paraphrased AICIS conclusion: the AICIS Evaluation Statement EVA00186 (26 June 2026) on Retinal (retinaldehyde, CAS 116-31-4), which is introduced in Australia for cosmetic use, concluded that retinal should be classified as a developmental toxicant (Reproductive toxicity Category 1B, may damage the unborn child), because it is the immediate metabolic precursor of retinoic acid and is expected to be more potent than retinol or retinyl esters. AICIS determined that retinal can no longer be introduced for consumer or cosmetic end use as an exempted or reported introduction; future cosmetic use needs an assessed introduction with risk management. The EU retinol and retinyl ester limits (Regulation 2024/996) do not cover retinaldehyde. Paraphrased factual summary; no verbatim text is stored.","source_locator":"AICIS Evaluation Statement EVA00186, Retinal (Retinaldehyde), 26 June 2026 (draft published 1 April 2026).","citation_text":"Australian Industrial Chemicals Introduction Scheme. Evaluation statement EVA00186: Retinal (Retinaldehyde). 26 June 2026.","claim_category":"ingredient-regulatory-safety-context","clinical_review_status":"pending: qualified review must reconfirm against the source before activation.","reuse_rights_status":"attribution_only","reuse_rights_basis":"Factual paraphrase with attribution and source link; protected expression not reproduced.","verification_status":"verified_paraphrase","verified_by":"web-assisted read of the AICIS evaluation statement (2026-09-23) — reviewer must reconfirm against the source PDF","verified_at":"2026-09-23","supersedes":"reg-retinal-status-v1","safety_flags":["developmental_toxicity_repr_1b_aicis_2026"],"product_specific":false,"attribution_required":true,"routine_selection_allowed":false,"ingredient_ids":["retinal"],"ingredient_names":["Retinal"],"inci_names":["Retinal"],"card_role":"grounding_evidence_only","selection_authority":"ingredient_governance_record","selection_authority_note":"routine_selection_allowed=false on this evidence card means the card itself can never authorise a routine. Whether an ingredient may be selected is decided by its governed ingredient record (approval_state and routine_selection_allowed in the knowledge-base database), not by any evidence card."}
{"document_id":"reg-mandelic-acid-status-v1","title":"Mandelic Acid — regulatory status factual card","source":"Regulatory status summary","source_family":"regulatory-status","source_url":"https://www.cir-safety.org/sites/default/files/ahas.pdf","published_year":"","document_type":"regulatory-safety-provenance-card","evidence_tier":"regulatory-safety-provenance","chunk_text":"Paraphrased regulatory status: Mandelic Acid is an alpha hydroxy acid but has no dedicated CIR or SCCS safety assessment (unlike glycolic and lactic acid, which are covered in the CIR AHA report). General AHA guidance applies — CIR recommended a maximum of about 10% AHA in consumer products, and AHAs increase UV sensitivity (sun protection advised). This card records the absence of a dedicated assessment plus general AHA context, not a mandelic-specific conclusion. Re-checked 2026-09-23: the CIR Alpha Hydroxy Acids report (final 1998, re-reviewed 2013) covers glycolic and lactic acids and their salts and esters only; mandelic acid is not among its covered ingredients, and no CIR or SCCS mandelic-acid assessment was located. Paraphrased factual summary; no verbatim text is stored.","source_locator":"No dedicated CIR/SCCS mandelic-acid assessment; general AHA guidance (CIR Alpha Hydroxy Acids report).","citation_text":"Regulatory status summary for Mandelic Acid; general AHA guidance from CIR Safety Assessment of Alpha Hydroxy Acids.","claim_category":"ingredient-regulatory-safety-context","clinical_review_status":"pending: qualified review must establish an appropriate source before activation.","reuse_rights_status":"attribution_only","reuse_rights_basis":"Factual status paraphrase with attribution; protected expression not reproduced.","verification_status":"verified_status_no_dedicated_assessment","verified_by":"web-search-assisted draft — reviewer to source a dedicated assessment","verified_at":"2026-09-23","product_specific":false,"attribution_required":true,"routine_selection_allowed":false,"ingredient_ids":["mandelic-acid"],"ingredient_names":["Mandelic Acid"],"inci_names":["Mandelic Acid"],"previous_verified_at":"2026-07-27","recheck_log":[{"date":"2026-09-23","checked":["CIR Alpha Hydroxy Acids report ingredient list (cir-safety.org/sites/default/files/ahas.pdf)","CIR findings listing"],"outcome":"no dedicated assessment located; status unchanged"}],"card_role":"grounding_evidence_only","selection_authority":"ingredient_governance_record","selection_authority_note":"routine_selection_allowed=false on this evidence card means the card itself can never authorise a routine. Whether an ingredient may be selected is decided by its governed ingredient record (approval_state and routine_selection_allowed in the knowledge-base database), not by any evidence card."}
